so let's start by first explaining what  is a blockchain  well a blockchain is just a data  structure that basically provides the  capabilities of a ledger so the  properties of a blockchain are  essentially three-fold the first one is  it provides this mechanism called  consensus which means that all the  participants in the system agree on what  data lives on the blockchain  unfortunately there's a bit of a caveat  to that and the caveat is that in fact  they agree on everything that's on the  blockchain except perhaps the last most  recent few entries that were added to  the chain  the second property is what we call  consent what we call persistence and  persistence basically means that once  data has been added to the blockchain it  can never be removed yeah and this is  kind of an extremely important property  of a blockchain once data has been added  there is no way to take it off  now again there's a caveat to that as  well  so this is a star star property that  basically says that in fact persistence  will only hold as long as we make  certain assumptions about the adversary  for example maybe the adversary only has  limited computing power  the third property that a blockchain  provides is what we call liveness which  means that in fact anyone can add data  to the blockchain so it's not possible  to prevent you from adding your own  transactions to the blockchain or it's  not possible for the blockchain to get  stuck so that no one can add new  transactions to it okay so i want you to  remember these three properties  consensus persistence and liveness  because those are going to be kind of  fundamental properties that the  blockchain as a data structure provides  to us and we're going to be building  applications on top of that i should  mention by the way if you want to see a  more formal definition of what a  blockchain is there's this nice paper  from 2016 that you can take a look at  and there's a much deeper analysis of  what a blockchain is and how do we argue  that consensus protocols actually  provide these these capabilities  okay so now that we understand what a  blockchain is  let's look at kind of the first  application for a blockchain which is a  currency yeah so how is the how does a  blockchain  provide a currency  well essentially as i said the fact that  we have consensus means that everybody  agrees on the set of transactions that  were already executed which means that  everybody agrees on how much money  everybody has yeah so all the  participants in the system sort of agree  on the current state of the world in  particular how much every participant  actually owns  persistence in the contents of a  currency means that once a transaction  has been executed there's no way to  revert that transaction yeah so in some  sense that's something that you often  want of the currency system in once in  the sense that once i pay you there's no  way for me to take the money back  yeah maybe you can give me the money  back but uh  as a change to the system there's no way  for me to kind of undo a transaction  that was already made  and liveness is kind of the the third  property which basically means the  system cannot get stuck and all of a  sudden not accept any more transactions  and moreover if a user uh wants to uh  submit a transaction to the system that  he will always have the ability to do it  so no one can prevent you from spending  the money that you already know own okay  so you can see how these properties of  consensus persistence and liveness um  immediately translates to providing a  currency and we'll talk more about that  later on but you already see why these  kind of properties are kind of  interesting on their own right  okay  so now the question is how do we achieve  persistence  persistence is kind of a difficult  property to achieve in a computer system  because a computer system can always be  snapshotted and then after some  modifications have been made to the  state of the system you can always  revert back back to the original  snapshot position yeah and that would  kind of undo everything that was done  after the snapshot was taken so the  question is how do we achieve  persistence in a computing computing  environment and the answer is basically  persistence is achieved by replication  across many many different parties so in  fact the blockchain as a whole is  replicated across many players so for  example miners are the ones who write  new data to the blockchain but in  addition they also uh store the entire  history of the blockchain locally okay  so the entire blockchain is replicated  to all the miners in the system the  people who write data to the blockchain  uh end users will monitor the blockchain  the blockchain for data that affects  themselves so for example if we're  implementing a currency um an end user  might want to know how much money do i  currently have in this currency and to  do that they will monitor the blockchain  by replicating it and  [Music]  looking at how much money they actually  have  so again there are ways to make this  more efficient uh but overall end user  there are end users who actually  replicate the blockchain  and there are particular software  applications often called wallets that  actually can be used to say how much how  much money each user has again uh there  are wallets that don't need to replicate  the entire blockchain but at the same  time  there are definitely users who do  replicate the the entirety of it and  then there are service providers who do  analysis of the blockchain like just  understanding the rate of transactions  understanding different aspects of the  blockchain and again they replicate the  blockchain  in order to process it okay so the fact  that this data is replicated across so  many participants makes it very  difficult to revert to an old state  because essentially you would have to  rever revert  all the participants who have  replicated the data on their own  so yeah so the fact is the fact that  everything is replicated means everyone  can validate that all transactions uh in  fact were done correctly on the  blockchain uh and in fact if ever if  ever we find a transaction that's  invalid the block that contains that  transaction is simply gonna be dropped  yeah so there's no incentive to creating  um an invalid transaction because anyone  who validates it will just ignore it and  it's not going to propagate throughout  the system and again we'll see more of  that in just a little bit  yeah and the point is that uh consensus  means essentially everybody's looking at  the same data and because they've all  validated it we know that it's the same  valid data so everybody agrees on the  state of the blockchain uh and all and  presumably all the transactions that are  on the blockchain are all valid under  the rules of the blockchain protocol
 

